<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Fan Chen
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science,
Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
Email: <a href="mailto:fanchen@mit.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">fanchen@mit.edu</a>.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sinho Chewi
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Statistics and Data Science, Yale University.
Email: <a href="mailto:sinho.chewi@yale.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">sinho.chewi@yale.edu</a>.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jianfeng Lu
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Mathematics, Duke University.
Email: <a href="mailto:jianfeng@math.duke.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">jianfeng@math.duke.edu</a>.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Matthew S. Zhang
</span><span id="id4" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
Email: <a href="mailto:shuns436@mit.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">shuns436@mit.edu</a>.</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">August 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a new low-accuracy sampler, called <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo</em>, which combines Gaussian smoothing, Picard iteration, and higher-order discretization.
For a log-concave target <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\propto\exp(-V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\propto\exp(-V)</annotation></semantics></math> in dimension <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\prec\alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V\preceq\beta I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≺</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\prec\alpha I\preceq\nabla^{2}V\preceq\beta I</annotation></semantics></math>, with condition number <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\coloneqq\beta/\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\coloneqq\beta/\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, smoothed Picard HMC returns a sample with <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\alpha}\,W_{2}(\cdot,\pi)\leqslant\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\alpha}\,W_{2}(\cdot,\pi)\leqslant\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{2}+\kappa^{7/6}d^{1/6}/\varepsilon^{1/3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\kappa^{2}+\kappa^{7/6}d^{1/6}/\varepsilon^{1/3})</annotation></semantics></math> gradient queries.
We also prove stronger <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> bounds, and then develop an algorithmic framework, the recursive warm start generator, to upgrade these <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> bounds to stronger divergence guarantees.
This produces a warm start for the proximal bouncy particle sampler, introduced in a companion work, leading to a high-accuracy log-concave sampler with complexity <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}((\kappa^{7/6}d^{1/6}+\kappa^{1/2}d^{1/4})\operatorname{polylog}(1/\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>polylog</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}((\kappa^{7/6}d^{1/6}+\kappa^{1/2}d^{1/4})\operatorname{polylog}(1/\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
